From c6e2ed4c166d7db0a3f1ba8eae54348346cd6d72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LiBr <me@nvme0n1p.dev>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 10:58:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 03/57] Z 6III: hand Linux over when the RTOS is ready, not at
 reset

LaMainApp attaches to the share_mem directory the RTOS builds: sub_405B08
does memcmp(page + 8, "MASTER", 7) and returns NULL on mismatch, at which
point CIpcPorting_Initialize prints "...[105], error." and the IPC layer is
dead for the rest of that run.

Linux reaches userspace in about 3 seconds. The emulated RTOS needs minutes.
The stand-in started Linux at machine reset, so Linux always lost a race the
body never has -- on a camera the RTOS has published that directory long
before Linux userspace exists. Proved rather than assumed: killing LaMainApp
and re-running it once the RTOS was up makes the error disappear.

-machine li-wait-ipc (on by default) now holds CPU3 until that header
appears, and the hand-off goes through async_run_on_cpu because it can now
fire from a timer while the machine is running -- writing another vCPU's
env.pc from the timer thread produced a core fetching from 0x200.

Measured after: Linux still boots to a BusyBox prompt, and
CIpcPorting_Initialize no longer fails at all.

Also: z63-tools/guest_sh.py, which runs commands in the guest's shell over
the serial port. That is how all of the above was established -- /proc
showed LaMainApp with /dev/oscom_memhelper open and exactly one page of
share_mem mapped at a14c00000, which is what pointed at the attach rather
than at the mmap or the ioctl.

Correction recorded in plan.md: menu drawing is the RTOS's job, not
LaMainApp's, so the display path does not run through the Linux application
and this fix, while real, is not what opens it. The display's own state is
written up there: sub_402B20EC writes the output size and never runs, while
the display subsystem's event loop is alive and still entering new blocks at
99.7% of the way through a run -- progressing, not deadlocked, and simply
never given a mode-set event.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
